```shiploop-state
{
  "evidence_refs": ["S1", "S2"],
  "headline": "Intake mapped the service and tests to outcomes, defaults, and discovery questions.",
  "outcome": "done",
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "K1.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Organisers create events and people reserve seats through HTTP endpoints; the request does not identify a separate user interface."
    },
    {
      "id": "K1.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "An organiser can create an event with a positive seat count, and people can reserve its available seats; a further reservation is rejected when it is full."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "In scope: a small Python HTTP service for event creation and seat reservation, with tests. Out of scope by default: a graphical interface, deployment, accounts, payments, cancellation, and other event-management features."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Discovery must confirm the stated empty repository and any environment-provided behavior or constraints before implementation; no existing application behavior is identified in the request."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume JSON over HTTP: POST /events accepts {\"seats\": integer} and returns {\"id\": string, \"seats\": integer, \"remaining_seats\": integer}; POST /events/{id}/reservations accepts an empty JSON object, reserves one seat, and returns {\"event_id\": string, \"remaining_seats\": integer}. Event IDs are generated by the service. Assume python -m unittest discovers and runs the tests."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume both successful POSTs return 201. Return 400 with {\"error\": \"invalid request\"} for invalid input, 404 with {\"error\": \"event not found\"} for an unknown event, and 409 with {\"error\": \"event full\"} when no seats remain. Unexpected server failures return 500 with {\"error\": \"internal server error\"}."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "An event starts with its specified positive integer number of seats; each successful reservation consumes one seat, and reservations stop when none remain. Concurrent requests must not oversell."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Reject malformed JSON, missing or extra fields, non-integer or non-positive seat counts, and reservation bodies other than an empty JSON object. Treat booleans as invalid seat counts."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume the service owns event IDs, capacities, and remaining-seat counts in process memory, shared by requests to that process until it stops or restarts. This rules out persistence and shared state across service processes."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume anonymous callers can create events and reserve seats for any known event ID; no identity, personal data, or private user-specific state is required. Concurrent reservations are serialized so capacity cannot be exceeded; restart clears all events and reservations. This rules out access control and recovery across restarts."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Discovery must establish the available Python version and runtime constraints; assume Python 3 if the environment offers a suitable version."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume the Python standard library is sufficient for the service and tests; do not add third-party dependencies unless discovery establishes a need."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.3",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume a locally configurable host and port, with defaults of 127.0.0.1 and 8000, and no required environment variables or external files. Discovery must check whether the runtime imposes different binding requirements."
    },
    {
      "id": "K7.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Keep HTTP parsing and responses separate from event state and reservation rules so the rules and HTTP boundary can be tested independently."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume the standard-library unittest framework and python -m unittest; discovery must establish that the available runtime supports them."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Exercise event creation and reservation through real HTTP requests, including invalid input, unknown and full events, and concurrent attempts at the last seat; verify the resulting status codes, response bodies, and capacity."
    },
    {
      "id": "K9.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Deliver runnable Python service source and tests in the repository checkout, with instructions sufficient to start and test it locally. No deployed target or deployment access is named."
    },
    {
      "id": "K10.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume no numerical performance target or interface-polish requirement. Preserve seat capacity under concurrent requests and validate request data. Discovery must establish any environment-imposed runtime or security bounds."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Likely rejection risks are overselling, an unusable or inconsistent HTTP contract, tests that miss the real boundary, and the in-memory lifetime or anonymous access differing from the intended use."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "An early HTTP smoke test and a concurrent last-seat test would expose contract and capacity failures; inspecting runtime constraints and recording the state and access defaults would expose assumption gaps."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "S1 — Fact: deliver a small Python HTTP event-creation and seat-reservation service for organisers and people using HTTP; the named repository checkout is the delivery target and grants authority to make the result usable there, while deployment is not requested; assumptions: use the recorded JSON endpoints, anonymous callers, one seat per reservation, atomic capacity checks, and process-local state cleared on restart, which rule out identity-based privacy, multi-process sharing, and persistence; open discovery items: discovery must first inspect the empty-repository claim, available runtime, and any runtime-provided state, identity, binding, or security facilities, then resolve compatible versions and configuration before implementation and verification; no team, workspace, channel, internal site, document, private repository, owner, prior run, or ambiguous domain term is identified as a source lead, and any sensitive locator discovered belongs only in an authorized private store, never KNOWLEDGE/; risks include overselling and mismatched access or state-lifetime assumptions; the isolated source checkout remains at its snapshot until final return, when the delivered source becomes visible, and any deployed outcome would exist only after a separately authorized release. S2 — Fact: include tests; recorded default: use unittest and real HTTP boundary checks, with discovery first establishing the available Python runtime before the test command can be confirmed; tests become usable in the delivered checkout at final return."
}
